vailable evidence supports its processing and phase-transformation relevance, but does not establish specific operating parameters or product performance outcomes beyond those described. [Data: Entities (19, 100); Relationships (695)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Prior γ-grain structure influences downstream microstructure\",\"explanation\":\"Γ-GRAIN represents the austenite grain structure before deformation in the non-recrystallization region and is linked to α-grain size and upper bainite formation. Finish-rolling temperature is also identified as affecting γ-grain size. [Data: Entities (224); Relationships (44)]\\n\\nThe broader Γ entity describes how recrystallized, unrecrystallized, and partially recrystallized regions of austenite contribute to the mixed grain structure observed after transformation. This establishes grain morphology as a key intermediate between rolling practice and final microstructural development. [Data: Entities (100); Relationships (44, 695)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Alloying and microalloying are secondary process-control themes\",\"explanation\":\"Microalloying is connected to Steels through references concerning steel-focused processing, while molybdenum is described as a transformation-delaying element in steels containing the γ phase. These relationships indicate that alloy chemistry is considered alongside deformation and temperature in the community’s treatment of steel processing. [Data: Entities (1109, 1031); Relationships (1609, 1456)]\\n\\nMolybdenum-containing steels form a more specific alloy family associated with gas and oil industry applications, and molybdenum is explicitly identified as a constituent of that family. [Data: Entities (1131); Relationships (1641)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community has broad steel-processing relevance but limited nontechnical evidence\",\"explanation\":\"Steels are the common material context linking hot deformation, microalloying, and welding-related literature. Welding is connected to steels through the cit